  "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of $y=3x-3$ is a straight line, and two distinct points can determine any straight line.\n\nSo, given any two distinct values of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$-values by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=3x-3.$\nFor example,\n\n- if $x=0$ then $y=3(0)-3=-3,$ which gives the point $(0,-3),$ and\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=3(1)-3=0,$ which gives the point $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This gives the following graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14189776/assets/58731786b9a03b6b76a0a9fa0b91563d2b4a098c8a0e8b1d5ace5690d3ea2913.png)",
